11:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L1c",null,{"guide":{"date":"Night of June 26–morning of June 27, 1862","place":"Hanover County, Virginia, north of the Chickahominy River: from Beaver Dam Creek east to Boatswain’s Swamp, the Turkey Hill plateau, Gaines’s farm area, and the Cold Harbor crossroads; the wider decision also shifted the Federal base from White House Landing toward Harrison’s Landing on the James River","result":"Although Porter’s V Corps had repulsed the Confederate attacks at Beaver Dam Creek, McClellan ordered it four miles east to a new defensive position behind Boatswain’s Swamp and decided to abandon the Richmond & York River Railroad supply line in favor of a base on the James. Porter prepared to hold north of the Chickahominy while the rest of the Army of the Potomac remained south of the river. Lee pursued a plan based on the mistaken expectation that the Federals would defend Powhite Creek and the route to White House Landing; on the morning of June 27, he had to revise that plan after encountering Porter’s unanticipated position. The episode ends before the main Battle of Gaines’s Mill begins","facts":[{"label":"Episode scope","value":"The decisions, terrain, commands, and faulty assumptions that set up Gaines’s Mill","note":"The opening eyewitness readings preview the late-afternoon fighting, but the main narrative stops before the battle begins."},{"label":"Campaign date","value":"Friday, June 27, 1862","note":"Gaines’s Mill was the third of the Seven Days Battles and is also called First Cold Harbor. This installment covers the preceding night and the opening movements that morning."},{"label":"McClellan’s 1 a.m. order","value":"Porter was directed to leave Beaver Dam Creek and fall back about four miles","note":"The episode identifies roughly 1 a.m. on June 27 as the moment McClellan gave up the immediate effort to capture Richmond."},{"label":"Three strategic choices","value":"Protect the York River supply line, attack Richmond south of the Chickahominy, or change base to the James","note":"Porter and other officers urged an attack toward Richmond while V Corps held Lee north of the river. McClellan chose the third course."},{"label":"Porter’s understanding","value":"Hold north of the river “to the last extremity”","note":"According to the episode’s account of Porter’s later recollection, he believed McClellan meant to attack Richmond while V Corps held. McClellan instead intended the stand to cover the army’s withdrawal."},{"label":"Change of base","value":"Abandon White House Landing and move the army toward Harrison’s Landing","note":"Movable supplies at White House Landing were to go by water to the James. The phrase “change of base” was McClellan’s preferred description; in operational effect the army was retreating from Richmond."},{"label":"Conflicting assessments","value":"Lee anticipated McClellan’s caution; McClellan underestimated Lee’s appetite for risk","note":"The episode treats the failed Confederate attack at Mechanicsville as sufficient to make McClellan surrender the initiative. Claims that a Federal attack would have captured Richmond are counterfactual, not established fact."},{"label":"Porter’s position","value":"A defensive arc about 1½–1¾ miles long behind Boatswain’s Swamp","note":"National Park Service and episode measurements differ slightly in wording. Both describe an arc on high ground north of the Chickahominy with difficult approaches and useful fields of fire."},{"label":"Terrain","value":"A brush-lined, sluggish stream below a steep rise to the Turkey Hill plateau","note":"The plateau fell toward the Chickahominy on the south. The Watt, McGehee, and Adams houses stood on the high ground."},{"label":"Why “Gaines’s Mill”","value":"The Gaines property gave the battle its name, although most fighting occurred southeast of the mill","note":"The episode places Dr. William Gaines’s farm near Powhite Creek and his mill farther north, more than a mile from the principal battlefield."},{"label":"Confederate map problem","value":"Lee’s map showed Powhite Creek but omitted Boatswain’s Swamp","note":"The episode presents this omission as a major reason Lee expected a Federal line in the wrong place. Exact cartographic content varies among surviving maps; the diagram shows the episode’s stated contrast, not Lee’s original map."},{"label":"Federal deployment","value":"George Morell faced west; George Sykes faced north; George McCall remained in reserve","note":"The two forward lines followed the creek and slope, with artillery on the plateau crest. McCall’s Pennsylvania Reserves had fought heavily on June 26."},{"label":"Episode strength estimate","value":"A little more than 27,000 men in nine brigades initially available to Porter","note":"The National Park Service gives 27,000 before later reinforcements raised the force to about 34,000. Strength depends on time and counting method; this episode ends before tracing those later arrivals."},{"label":"Federal artillery","value":"17 batteries and 96 guns with V Corps","note":"The episode also gives William B. Franklin’s VI Corps three long-range batteries south of the Chickahominy that could fire across the river. Battery and gun totals can vary with time and attachment."},{"label":"Slocum checked","value":"Henry W. Slocum’s division was initially stopped before crossing to reinforce Porter","note":"The episode says McClellan recalled the division when reports suggested a Confederate attack south of the river. Elements of Slocum’s division did reinforce Porter later on June 27, beyond this installment’s endpoint."},{"label":"Magruder’s demonstration","value":"Troop movements, shouted orders, drums, and pressure along the Richmond front","note":"John B. Magruder sought to convince the Federal corps south of the Chickahominy that they faced a serious attack, helping keep them from being used more aggressively."},{"label":"Lee’s expected Federal response","value":"Defend Powhite Creek and the supply route toward White House Landing","note":"McClellan had instead decided to abandon that route. Porter occupied Boatswain’s Swamp, a stronger position east of where Lee expected to meet him."},{"label":"Confederate morning concept","value":"Jackson and D. H. Hill move toward Old Cold Harbor while A. P. Hill and Longstreet press the retreating Federals east","note":"Lee expected the outside columns to get beyond the Federal right and receive troops driven from Powhite Creek. The arrows in the diagram are generalized operational relationships, not exact routes."},{"label":"Command uncertainty","value":"Lee began June 27 still trying to locate Jackson and interpret the Federal withdrawal","note":"The episode says Lee sent an aide to find Jackson after first light. Confederate pickets then reported that the Beaver Dam Creek line was empty."},{"label":"Leader casualties in scope","value":"None in the narrated setup to the battle","note":"Porter’s Mexican War wound is biographical background. The opening readings describe later combat at Gaines’s Mill but do not establish a killed or wounded senior commander for this episode’s losses section."},{"label":"Recommended book","value":"Seven Days Battles 1862: Lee’s Defense of Richmond by Angus Konstam","note":"The episode describes it as an introductory volume in Osprey Publishing’s Campaign series. The automated transcript renders the author’s surname as “Constam.”"},{"label":"Transcript normalizations","value":"Fitz John Porter, Boatswain’s Swamp, Powhite Creek, McGehee House, and Philip St. George Cooke","note":"The automated transcript variously renders these as “Fitch,” “Boats/Boesens,” “Powhat,” “McGeehee,” and “Cook.”"}],"maps":[{"title":"The change of base after Mechanicsville","caption":"Porter withdrew east from Beaver Dam Creek to Boatswain’s Swamp. McClellan left the Federal majority south of the Chickahominy, gave up the railroad and White House Landing, and directed the supply system toward Harrison’s Landing on the James. Rivers, roads, positions, and movement paths are generalized. This map is schematic and not to scale.","source":"National Park Service, U.S. Army Center of Military History, U.S. Army Transportation Corps, and episode transcript; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep159-change-of-base-overview.svg","alt":"Schematic overview of Porter’s withdrawal and McClellan’s change of supply base from the York River to the James River."},{"title":"The position Lee expected and the position Porter occupied","caption":"Lee expected a Federal defense behind Powhite Creek and sent Jackson and D. H. Hill toward Old Cold Harbor to turn it. Porter instead formed an arc behind Boatswain’s Swamp, with Morell facing west, Sykes north, McCall in reserve, and artillery on the crest. The episode says Lee’s map omitted Boatswain’s Swamp. All terrain, formations, routes, and distances are generalized. This map is schematic and not to scale.","source":"National Park Service, U.S. Army Center of Military History, Robert E. Lee’s official report, and episode transcript; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep159-porter-position-schematic.svg","alt":"Schematic comparison of Lee’s expected Federal line at Powhite Creek with Porter’s actual position behind Boatswain’s Swamp."},{"title":"Robert Knox Sneden’s contemporary-retrospective battle plan","caption":"Sneden’s pen-and-ink and watercolor map shows Powhite Creek, Cold Harbor, the Chickahominy, and color-coded forces. Its title dates the battle June 26, but the fighting occurred June 27. The map was made during or soon after the war and is a retrospective depiction, not the Confederate map Lee used on the morning of battle.","source":"Library of Congress Geography and Map Division and Virginia Historical Society; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep159-gaines-mill-sneden-map.jpg","alt":"Robert Knox Sneden’s color plan of the Battle of Gaines’s Mill, incorrectly titled as fought June 26, 1862."}],"moments":[{"time":"0:13","audioTime":13,"title":"A cavalryman recalls the battle’s final crisis","detail":"Private William H. Hitchcock of the 5th U.S. Cavalry describes Philip St. George Cooke ordering a charge to help Federal artillery withdraw. This reading previews fighting covered in later installments."},{"time":"3:17","audioTime":197,"title":"A Texan recalls Hood’s breakthrough","detail":"John W. Stevens of the 5th Texas describes the assault over the Federal works and against the reserve guns. The excerpt is a participant’s later account, not the start of the chronological narrative."},{"time":"5:47","audioTime":347,"title":"Episode 159 begins","detail":"The hosts resume the Seven Days after Oak Grove and Mechanicsville and shift to the setup for Gaines’s Mill."},{"time":"6:12","audioTime":372,"title":"Lee gains the initiative despite defeat","detail":"McClellan orders Porter out of the Beaver Dam Creek position that had repulsed the Confederate attacks."},{"time":"6:35","audioTime":395,"title":"The 1 a.m. withdrawal order","detail":"The episode places McClellan’s decisive order around 1 a.m. on June 27: Porter will fall back four miles to Boatswain’s Swamp."},{"time":"7:09","audioTime":427,"title":"Jackson is still on Porter’s flank","detail":"Reports correct McClellan’s earlier belief that Jackson had been defeated and warn that the Valley command threatens V Corps from the north."},{"time":"7:34","audioTime":454,"title":"McClellan’s alternatives are stated","detail":"He can defend the railroad supply line or hold Lee north of the river while attacking Richmond with the army’s main body to the south."},{"time":"7:57","audioTime":477,"title":"Porter urges an attack on Richmond","detail":"Porter recommends enough reinforcement to hold north of the river while the larger Federal force attacks south of it."},{"time":"8:42","audioTime":522,"title":"Porter is told to hold","detail":"McClellan stresses the necessity of holding until arrangements across the river are complete, without clearly telling Porter that the army will retreat."},{"time":"9:23","audioTime":563,"title":"McClellan chooses to save the army","detail":"The episode says he rejects the proposed attack and informs his staff, but not his field commanders, that the army is retreating."},{"time":"10:05","audioTime":605,"title":"V Corps must buy one more day","detail":"Porter is to resist on June 27 and then cross to the south side of the Chickahominy that night."},{"time":"10:33","audioTime":633,"title":"White House Landing is abandoned","detail":"McClellan directs movable supplies to be loaded on ships for Harrison’s Landing on the James River."},{"time":"11:09","audioTime":669,"title":"The commanders read one another differently","detail":"Lee correctly expects caution; McClellan’s earlier view that Lee would avoid a bold movement has already failed."},{"time":"12:25","audioTime":745,"title":"Boatswain’s Swamp offers a strong defense","detail":"The episode describes the swampy stream, steep rise, plateau, and Chickahominy behind Porter’s new line."},{"time":"13:01","audioTime":781,"title":"Fitz John Porter’s career","detail":"The narrative reviews his naval family, West Point class of 1845, Mexican War service, prewar staff work, Texas mission, and rise to V Corps command."},{"time":"14:43","audioTime":883,"title":"The Watt House becomes headquarters","detail":"Porter uses the house on Turkey Hill; the episode notes that the seventy-seven-year-old Sarah Watt left for safety."},{"time":"14:54","audioTime":894,"title":"Lee’s map omits Boatswain’s Swamp","detail":"The map available to Lee showed nearby Powhite Creek but not the wooded watercourse screening Porter’s actual line."},{"time":"15:45","audioTime":945,"title":"Porter forms the archer’s bow","detail":"Morell’s division faces west and Sykes’s division north, with two infantry lines and a third artillery line along the crest."},{"time":"16:29","audioTime":989,"title":"Ninety-six guns support V Corps","detail":"Porter has seventeen batteries; Franklin’s guns south of the river can also reach Confederate approaches from the west."},{"time":"17:00","audioTime":1021,"title":"Slocum’s reinforcement is stopped","detail":"McClellan checks the division at the bridge because he fears a simultaneous Confederate attack south of the Chickahominy."},{"time":"17:47","audioTime":1067,"title":"Magruder sustains the deception","detail":"Movement, shouted orders, and drums strengthen Federal reports of a threat on the Richmond front."},{"time":"18:47","audioTime":1127,"title":"Lee begins correcting the previous day’s failures","detail":"After first light he sends an aide to find Jackson and waits for news of the Federal position."},{"time":"19:06","audioTime":1146,"title":"Beaver Dam Creek is empty","detail":"Confederate pickets discover the abandoned works, and Lee’s columns begin moving after Porter."},{"time":"19:31","audioTime":1172,"title":"Lee’s plan rests on the wrong response","detail":"He expects McClellan to defend the supply line and Porter to make a stand at Powhite Creek."},{"time":"20:16","audioTime":1217,"title":"The intended trap","detail":"Jackson is to pass beyond the Federal right while A. P. Hill and Longstreet drive the expected Powhite Creek line toward Jackson and D. H. Hill."},{"time":"20:50","audioTime":1250,"title":"Two assumptions fail","detail":"McClellan will not protect White House Landing, and Porter is entrenched on stronger high ground behind a stream missing from Lee’s map."},{"time":"21:09","audioTime":1269,"title":"Lee must revise in real time","detail":"The episode ends its operational narrative with the Confederate commander moving into a situation different from the one he planned for."},{"time":"21:34","audioTime":1294,"title":"The book recommendation","detail":"The hosts recommend Angus Konstam’s Seven Days Battles 1862: Lee’s Defense of Richmond as an introduction to the campaign."}],"command":[{"side":"United States","color":"#315f8c","branches":[{"commander":"Maj. Gen. George B. McClellan","command":"Army of the Potomac","subordinates":"Ordered Porter east, rejected an immediate attack on Richmond, abandoned the York River supply line, and began the change of base to the James"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. Fitz John Porter","command":"V Corps north of the Chickahominy","subordinates":"George W. Morell’s division faced west; George Sykes’s division faced north; George A. McCall’s Pennsylvania Reserves formed the reserve"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. William B. Franklin","command":"VI Corps south of the Chickahominy","subordinates":"Had long-range artillery able to support Porter; Henry W. Slocum’s division was initially checked at the bridge while McClellan assessed threats south of the river"},{"commander":"Maj. Gens. Edwin V. Sumner, Samuel P. Heintzelman, and Erasmus D. Keyes","command":"II, III, and IV Corps south of the Chickahominy","subordinates":"Remained on the Richmond front while reports of Confederate activity reinforced McClellan’s fear of an attack there"}]},{"side":"Confederacy","color":"#9a3d32","branches":[{"commander":"Gen. Robert E. Lee","command":"Army of Northern Virginia","subordinates":"Pursued Porter while still expecting the Federals to defend Powhite Creek and the route to White House Landing"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Thomas J. “Stonewall” Jackson","command":"Left wing moving toward Old Cold Harbor","subordinates":"Lee sent an aide to find him after first light; his movement with D. H. Hill was intended to pass beyond the expected Federal right"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. D. H. Hill","command":"Division supporting Jackson’s movement","subordinates":"Moved toward Old Cold Harbor as part of the force Lee expected to place beyond the Federal flank"},{"commander":"Maj. Gens. A. P. Hill and James Longstreet","command":"Pursuit east from the Mechanicsville sector","subordinates":"Expected to push a Powhite Creek defense toward Jackson and D. H. Hill; instead their advance would encounter Porter at Boatswain’s Swamp"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. John B. Magruder","command":"Richmond front south of the Chickahominy","subordinates":"Used conspicuous movements and noise to reinforce the appearance of an impending attack against the Federal main body"}]}],"people":[{"name":"Fitz John Porter","role":"V Corps commander who selected and organized the Boatswain’s Swamp position","side":"United States","image":"/images/ep159-fitz-john-porter.jpg"},{"name":"George B. McClellan","role":"Army of the Potomac commander who ordered the change of base to the James","side":"United States","image":"/images/ep159-george-mcclellan.jpg"},{"name":"Robert E. Lee","role":"Confederate army commander whose June 27 plan assumed a Federal stand at Powhite Creek","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Thomas J. “Stonewall” Jackson","role":"Confederate wing commander directed toward Old Cold Harbor and the expected Federal flank","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"John B. Magruder","role":"Confederate commander whose demonstration helped hold the Federal main body south of the Chickahominy","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"George W. Morell","role":"Federal division commander assigned the west-facing sector of Porter’s line","side":"United States"},{"name":"George Sykes","role":"Federal division commander assigned the north-facing sector of Porter’s line","side":"United States"},{"name":"George A. McCall","role":"Commander of the Pennsylvania Reserves, placed in reserve after the fighting at Beaver Dam Creek","side":"United States"},{"name":"Henry W. Slocum","role":"VI Corps division commander whose movement to reinforce Porter was initially stopped","side":"United States"},{"name":"William B. Franklin","role":"VI Corps commander south of the river whose long-range batteries could support Porter","side":"United States"},{"name":"D. H. Hill","role":"Confederate division commander moving with Jackson toward Old Cold Harbor","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"A. P. Hill","role":"Confederate division commander pursuing east from the Mechanicsville sector","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"James Longstreet","role":"Confederate division commander assigned to support the pursuit east","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Philip St. George Cooke","role":"Federal cavalry commander heard in the opening eyewitness account ordering a late-day charge to protect the guns","side":"United States"}],"losses":[],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/ep159-change-of-base-overview.svg","alt":"Schematic overview of the change of base from White House Landing to Harrison’s Landing.","caption":"McClellan ordered Porter east to Boatswain’s Swamp and shifted the army’s logistical base from the York River route toward the James. Routes and positions are generalized.","source":"National Park Service, U.S. Army Center of Military History, U.S. Army Transportation Corps, and episode transcript; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep159-porter-position-schematic.svg","alt":"Schematic comparison of Powhite Creek with Porter’s position behind Boatswain’s Swamp.","caption":"Lee expected a line behind Powhite Creek. Porter instead occupied the plateau beyond Boatswain’s Swamp. The arrows and formations show generalized relationships, not exact geometry.","source":"National Park Service, U.S. Army Center of Military History, Robert E. Lee’s official report, and episode transcript; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep159-gaines-mill-sneden-map.jpg","alt":"Robert Knox Sneden’s color plan of Gaines’s Mill.","caption":"Sneden’s map is valuable as a contemporary-retrospective image of the battlefield, but its title gives June 26 rather than the correct battle date of June 27.","source":"Library of Congress Geography and Map Division and Virginia Historical Society; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep159-waud-gaines-mill.jpg","alt":"Alfred Waud’s field sketch of the Battle of Gaines’s Mill.","caption":"Alfred R. Waud’s drawing depicts the battle the episode’s two opening eyewitness accounts preview. It illustrates later fighting, not the morning setup that forms the chronological endpoint of this installment.","source":"National Park Service reproduction; original drawing credited there to the Library of Congress; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep159-gaines-mill-ruins.jpg","alt":"John Reekie’s April 1865 photograph of the ruins of Gaines’s Mill.","caption":"John Reekie photographed the ruined mill in April 1865; Alexander Gardner published the image. The structure gave the 1862 battle its name even though the principal combat took place more than a mile away.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; no known restrictions on publication; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep159-fitz-john-porter.jpg","alt":"Civil War-era glass-negative portrait of Fitz John Porter in uniform.","caption":"Porter, thirty-nine years old during the campaign, commanded V Corps and prepared the defensive line behind Boatswain’s Swamp.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; no known restrictions on publication; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep159-george-mcclellan.jpg","alt":"Civil War-era glass-negative portrait of George B. McClellan.","caption":"McClellan chose to abandon the York River supply line and ordered Porter to hold while arrangements for the move toward the James were made.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; no known restrictions on publication; see sources below."}],"sources":[{"label":"Official Civil War Podcast episode 159 page","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/2016/07/24/civil-war-podcast-episode-159/"},{"label":"National Park Service: The Battle of Gaines’s Mill","url":"https://www.nps.gov/rich/learn/historyculture/gainesmillbull.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service battle detail: Gaines’s Mill (VA017)","url":"https://www.nps.gov/civilwar/search-battles-detail.htm?battleCode=va017"},{"label":"National Park Service Civil War Series: The Battles for Richmond, 1862","url":"https://npshistory.com/publications/civil_war_series/21/sec5.htm"},{"label":"U.S. Army Center of Military History: The Virginia Campaigns, March–August 1862","url":"https://history.army.mil/portals/143/Images/Publications/catalog/75-5.pdf"},{"label":"U.S. Army Transportation Corps: White House Landing","url":"https://transportation.army.mil/History/whitehouselanding.html"},{"label":"Primary source collection: Official Records, Series I, Volume XI, Part II","url":"https://ehistory.osu.edu/books/official-records/013"},{"label":"Primary source: Robert E. Lee’s official report on the Seven Days Battles","url":"https://www.battlefields.org/learn/primary-sources/robert-e-lees-report-seven-days-battles"},{"label":"Cornell University Library: McClellan’s official campaign report","url":"https://digital.library.cornell.edu/catalog/may927011"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Robert Knox Sneden plan of Gaines’s Mill","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/gvhs01.vhs00217/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: ruins of Gaines’s Mill by Reekie and Gardner","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2007685829/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Fitz John Porter portrait","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018667594/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: George B. McClellan portrait","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018669440/"},{"label":"Official podcast book-recommendations list","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/book-recommendations/"}]}}]}]
