orter"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"George W. Morell’s division faced west; George Sykes’s division faced north; George A. McCall’s Pennsylvania Reserves formed the reserve"}]]}],["$","div","VI Corps south of the Chickahominy-Brig. Gen. William B. Franklin",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"VI Corps south of the Chickahominy"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Brig. Gen. William B. Franklin"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Had long-range artillery able to support Porter; Henry W. Slocum’s division was initially checked at the bridge while McClellan assessed threats south of the river"}]]}],["$","div","II, III, and IV Corps south of the Chickahominy-Maj. Gens. Edwin V. Sumner, Samuel P. Heintzelman, and Erasmus D. Keyes",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"II, III, and IV Corps south of the Chickahominy"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Maj. Gens. Edwin V. Sumner, Samuel P. Heintzelman, and Erasmus D. Keyes"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Remained on the Richmond front while reports of Confederate activity reinforced McClellan’s fear of an attack there"}]]}]]]}],["$","section","Confederacy",{"className":"command-column #9a3d32","children":[["$","h3",null,{"children":"Confederacy"}],[["$","div","Army of Northern Virginia-Gen. Robert E. Lee",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Army of Northern Virginia"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Gen. Robert E. Lee"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Pursued Porter while still expecting the Federals to defend Powhite Creek and the route to White House Landing"}]]}],["$","div","Left wing moving toward Old Cold Harbor-Maj. Gen. Thomas J. “Stonewall” Jackson",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Left wing moving toward Old Cold Harbor"}],"$L1d","$L1e"]}],"$L1f","$L20","$L21"]]}]]}],"$L22"]]]}]
14:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section","id":"images","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"Historical images"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"history-gallery","children":[["$","$L23","/images/ep159-gaines-mill-sneden-map.jpg",{"image":"/images/ep159-gaines-mill-sneden-map.jpg","alt":"Robert Knox Sneden’s color plan of Gaines’s Mill.","caption":"Sneden’s map is valuable as a contemporary-retrospective image of the battlefield, but its title gives June 26 rather than the correct battle date of June 27.","name":"Robert Knox Sneden’s color plan of Gaines’s Mill.","source":"$undefined","credit":"Library of Congress Geography and Map Division and Virginia Historical Society; see sources below."}],["$","$L23","/images/ep159-waud-gaines-mill.jpg",{"image":"/images/ep159-waud-gaines-mill.jpg","alt":"Alfred Waud’s field sketch of the Battle of Gaines’s Mill.","caption":"Alfred R. Waud’s drawing depicts the battle the episode’s two opening eyewitness accounts preview. It illustrates later fighting, not the morning setup that forms the chronological endpoint of this installment.","name":"Alfred Waud’s field sketch of the Battle of Gaines’s Mill.","source":"$undefined","credit":"National Park Service reproduction; original drawing credited there to the Library of Congress; see sources below."}],["$","$L23","/images/ep159-gaines-mill-ruins.jpg",{"image":"/images/ep159-gaines-mill-ruins.jpg","alt":"John Reekie’s April 1865 photograph of the ruins of Gaines’s Mill.","caption":"John Reekie photographed the ruined mill in April 1865; Alexander Gardner published the image. The structure gave the 1862 battle its name even though the principal combat took place more than a mile away.","name":"John Reekie’s April 1865 photograph of the ruins of Gaines’s Mill.","source":"$undefined","credit":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; no known restrictions on publication; see sources below."}],["$","$L23","/images/ep159-fitz-john-porter.jpg",{"image":"/images/